sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch06-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Although class 4 is the most complex in appearance, in terms of overall activity it is intermediate between class 2 and class 3, and in sequences of rules it commonly appears at the transition between class 2 and class 3 rules.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Class 4 lies between class 2 and class 3 in activity";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 6, “Starting from Randomness”; section “Four Classes of Behavior” (from p. 231), p. 242. Scope: sequences of cellular automaton rules, both discrete and continuous"
}
